<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Vickery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Vickery was an American historian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. He wrote widely on early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> history, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> period in Cambodia 1975–1982, and on Thai history. In the late 1980s he was among the scholars who questioned the authenticity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> inscription, the founding text of Sukhothai history.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michael Theodore Vickery was born on 1 April 1931 in Billings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a>. He took a Bachelor of Arts in Russian studies at the University of Washington in 1952, spent 1953 to 1955 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> as a Fulbright scholar and served in the United States Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1956 to 1958. He then taught English in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> from 1958 to 1960, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> from 1960 to 1964 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> from 1964 to 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He did his thesis research in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> from 1970 to 1972 and in 1973 became a lecturer in Southeast Asian history at the Universiti Sains Malaysia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a>. In 1977 he received his doctorate in history from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale University</a> for Cambodia After Angkor: The Chronicular Evidence for the Fourteenth to Sixteenth Centuries.</p><p>He was then a research fellow at the Australian National University from 1979 to 1982 and at the University of Adelaide from 1982 to 1988, a senior lecturer again at the Universiti Sains Malaysia from 1988 to 1998, and taught Cambodian history at the Royal University of Fine Arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> from 1998 to 2002. After working as an independent scholar in Chiang Mai Province from 2002 to 2008, he became a visiting professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-sydney">University of Sydney</a>.</p><p>He also wrote columns for the Phnom Penh Post from 1992 to 2007, and in 2008 it was announced that he had worked as an analyst and historical expert for the defence team of Ieng Sary, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> foreign minister, at the Khmer Rouge Tribunal. He died of a heart attack in Battambang Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, on 29 June 2017, at the age of 86.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His work on pre-Angkorian, Angkorian and post-Angkor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> drew on newly deciphered inscriptions and brought in the views of the Cambodian scholars Khin Sok and Mak Phoen. He argued that much earlier scholarship could be overturned by a single new inscription, and remarked that studying Cambodian history with Georges Coédès was like doing geography with Ptolemy. His books include Cambodia 1975–1982 (1984), on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a> era and its aftermath, and Society, Economics and Politics in Pre-Angkor Cambodia (1998). Some scholars placed his work in Marxist schools of thought, though he was not a communist, and he wrote for the Bulletin of Concerned Asian Scholars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael Vickery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chenla, or Zhenla, is the name the Chinese gave to an entity that sent tribute to their emperors; neither it nor Funan is known in Old Khmer, and <b>Michael Vickery</b> holds the original meanings of both unknown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people's ethnicity is debated: <b>Michael Vickery</b> holds that on present evidence they can only have been Khmer, Vietnamese scholars such as Mac Duong think them Austronesian, and the only writings of the period are Sanskrit inscriptions in Pallava Grantha script, which say nothing of the vernacular.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 1980s scholars including <b>Michael Vickery</b> and Piriya Krairiksh questioned whether the inscription was a later composition, possibly of the nineteenth century, and the debate remains unresolved, though many specialists accept it as substantially of the period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Billings, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battambang">Battambang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.79,-108.54&amp;year=1986" aria-label="Billings on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Michael Vickery"><image href="/maps/globes/g240n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5679 6144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5679 6144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.79,-108.54&amp;year=1986">What was here in 1986? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
